WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.501 --> 00:00:04.471
The Coronavirus outbreak hit people across the world,

00:00:04.938 --> 00:00:06.840
claiming too many lives.

00:00:07.107 --> 00:00:09.776
It infected the very roots of our economy

00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:13.279
and raised many questions 
about our health care systems.

00:00:14.447 --> 00:00:16.383
In this first episode of "What's Left?"

00:00:16.383 --> 00:00:19.753
we take a closer look at the spread
of the crisis to the economy.

00:00:19.886 --> 00:00:21.688
I hope that we will learn some lessons.

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:24.224
In 2008, we had a major crisis.

00:00:24.224 --> 00:00:25.892
Everyone said: "we're going to change everything, 

00:00:25.892 --> 00:00:26.860
we're going to change everything".

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:28.161
We clearly see that nothing has changed.

00:00:28.328 --> 00:00:30.563
This crisis might just be the opportunity

00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:32.866
to rethink Europe's current economic model.

00:00:33.466 --> 00:00:34.134
And here's how.

00:00:52.986 --> 00:00:55.889
As countries start deconfining one after the other, 

00:00:56.122 --> 00:00:59.359
economic figures are out and they're not reassuring.

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.262
The global freeze of the economy

00:01:02.262 --> 00:01:03.596
- a first in modern history -

00:01:04.264 --> 00:01:08.535
is set to cause the Eurozone to contract by 7.5% this year,

00:01:08.968 --> 00:01:11.237
the worst recession since the Great Depression.

00:01:11.438 --> 00:01:13.673
As long as we don't have a remedy, a vaccine,

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.342
this sword of Damocles hangs over our heads.

00:01:16.342 --> 00:01:21.481
It generates uncertainty in margins that we have never seen.

00:01:21.681 --> 00:01:24.484
So now the question is, what will happen after confinement?

00:01:25.185 --> 00:01:28.688
The jobs of up to 59 million people in Europe are at risk;

00:01:29.322 --> 00:01:32.025
that's almost the entire population of France.

00:01:32.258 --> 00:01:36.229
Beyond the numbers, we are talking
about the livelihoods of citizens,

00:01:36.229 --> 00:01:40.500
their incomes and their plans for the future
that were put in question overnight. 

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:43.703
There are have been legislative and budgetary actions

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:46.573
by member states since the beginning of the crisis.

00:01:46.973 --> 00:01:50.276
However, these measures will unevenly affect member states

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:52.479
due to the different spread of the virus,

00:01:52.479 --> 00:01:54.147
but also due to the different structures

00:01:54.147 --> 00:01:56.116
of the economies of those states.

00:01:56.182 --> 00:01:58.718
It took a month of confinement before EU leaders

00:01:58.718 --> 00:02:01.521
finally took action as one united block. 

00:02:01.521 --> 00:02:05.325
A common "bazooka" package amounting
to €540 billion,

00:02:05.325 --> 00:02:07.861
was agreed on in April to protect workers,

00:02:07.861 --> 00:02:08.761
businesses 

00:02:08.761 --> 00:02:09.729
and countries.

00:02:10.163 --> 00:02:12.732
This is a European crisis, not that of one single State.

00:02:12.732 --> 00:02:14.200
and it must be addressed as such. 

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.370
Therefore, the costs have to be shared.

00:02:17.837 --> 00:02:20.840
In the longer term, all EU countries will need cash

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:23.076
to face the economic consequences of the crisis,

00:02:23.243 --> 00:02:26.212
in other words finance a massive recovery plan.

00:02:27.147 --> 00:02:28.815
If we take a look at history,

00:02:28.815 --> 00:02:31.618
we may find some clues to figure out what to do next.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.723
In the 1930s, to pull his country out of the Depression,

00:02:37.357 --> 00:02:39.926
Roosevelt decided to take radical measures.

00:02:40.994 --> 00:02:43.129
He tripled the federal budget in a few years,

00:02:43.229 --> 00:02:44.264
created new resources,

00:02:44.297 --> 00:02:47.167
putting in place a federal tax on profits
and on the richest.

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:49.636
The first year it didn't work out.

00:02:49.969 --> 00:02:51.804
Roosevelt emitted bonds for a few years

00:02:51.804 --> 00:02:54.807
and with a massive stimulus package
his tax plan paid off.

00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:58.811
For today's Progressives

00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:01.514
this precedent in history could give us a way out. 

00:03:04.017 --> 00:03:07.053
One tool governments have in their toolbox
to finance their actions,

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:08.955
in addition to taxation, is debt.

00:03:09.956 --> 00:03:10.990
In normal times,

00:03:11.024 --> 00:03:13.226
all European countries can issue bonds

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:15.328
to borrow money on the financial markets.

00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:18.765
But at some point those countries
will have to reimburse the money.

00:03:19.365 --> 00:03:21.935
If financial markets trust your country,

00:03:21.935 --> 00:03:23.336
they will lend money easily,

00:03:23.503 --> 00:03:25.004
at low interest rates.

00:03:25.004 --> 00:03:27.006
The first thing was to say:

00:03:27.006 --> 00:03:29.876
"there are countries that are going
to be more affected than others

00:03:29.876 --> 00:03:32.545
by this health crisis."

00:03:33.012 --> 00:03:34.914
So there are countries
that will have more public debt than others.

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:37.550
But for other countries that don't look quite as stable,

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.052
interest rates can rise.

00:03:39.052 --> 00:03:41.955
Financial markets can say: 
"Ah, but you are less trusted!

00:03:41.955 --> 00:03:45.358
So we're going to ask you for interest rates at 2% or 3%.

00:03:45.358 --> 00:03:48.428
This has a tremendous impact on a country's ability

00:03:48.428 --> 00:03:50.296
to restore its economic strength.

00:03:50.530 --> 00:03:53.766
What might seem like a pretty insignificant
rise in interest rates

00:03:53.766 --> 00:03:57.203
can quite quickly turn into billions
of Euros to be paid back.

00:03:57.203 --> 00:04:00.807
When we anticipate that one country
will be more affected than another,

00:04:00.807 --> 00:04:02.342
can we arbitrate its public debt against

00:04:02.342 --> 00:04:03.910
that of a country that would be less affected?

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:05.812
If this arbitration mechanism occurs,

00:04:05.812 --> 00:04:07.647
we have a sort of hellish circle

00:04:07.647 --> 00:04:09.983
which leads to penalising the most affected countries.

00:04:10.049 --> 00:04:13.152
It's a totally unfair mechanism.

00:04:13.152 --> 00:04:16.256
It is a reverse solidarity. An anti solidarity.

00:04:17.624 --> 00:04:20.093
In order to avoid this competition between states

00:04:20.093 --> 00:04:23.263
and make sure we have a truly solidarity-based recovery,

00:04:23.263 --> 00:04:25.331
Progressives in the European Parliament

00:04:25.331 --> 00:04:29.102
put the idea of issuing
corona bonds at a European level on the table.

00:04:29.369 --> 00:04:32.272
In other words, a common European debt.

00:04:34.574 --> 00:04:37.210
Each country would keep its debt capacity,

00:04:37.277 --> 00:04:39.812
but Europe, as such, would also
have the capacity to generate debt.

00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:41.481
The financial markets would take it as

00:04:41.481 --> 00:04:43.750
the signature of the best of them.

00:04:43.750 --> 00:04:47.820
Some people call it "corona bonds",
because it's somehow linked to the coronavirus crisis 

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:50.590
but it would not only concern the health aspect.

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:52.392
It would also help to finance the recovery plan.

00:04:54.661 --> 00:04:58.031
If the EU jointly borrows money on the financial markets,

00:04:58.031 --> 00:05:01.567
it also needs to be able to jointly
pay back money borrowed.

00:05:02.268 --> 00:05:05.438
The Progressives are pushing to create new own resources

00:05:05.438 --> 00:05:08.641
for the EU budget to create independent
revenue for the EU. 

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:13.179
Asking for a fee on un-recycled plastics,

00:05:13.179 --> 00:05:16.449
drawing resources from the European Emissions Trade System, 

00:05:16.449 --> 00:05:19.185
raising income taxes on big corporates,

00:05:19.185 --> 00:05:21.220
creating a digital services tax,

00:05:21.354 --> 00:05:23.956
putting in place a tax on financial transactions,

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:27.226
while making sure the revenues from tax avoidance
and tax evasion

00:05:27.226 --> 00:05:28.728
go where the money is needed.

00:05:33.733 --> 00:05:35.435
A few weeks into confinement,

00:05:35.435 --> 00:05:38.538
people woke up to a world where the air was clean

00:05:38.604 --> 00:05:40.640
and where traffic wasn't a nuisance anymore.

00:05:41.274 --> 00:05:44.077
Spectacular drops in CO2 emissions were observed.

00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:47.146
But this doesn't give us the bigger picture.

00:05:48.114 --> 00:05:49.115
In the last year,

00:05:49.315 --> 00:05:52.452
C02 emissions have risen more rapidly than in the 60s and 70s,

00:05:52.452 --> 00:05:55.588
from 0,8 units to 2,8 per year.

00:05:56.222 --> 00:05:58.291
Temperatures continue to rise everywhere,

00:05:58.458 --> 00:06:01.160
and with it our need to deal with the situation.

00:06:02.261 --> 00:06:03.563
We just lived the horror

00:06:03.730 --> 00:06:05.765
but at least there was tap water.

00:06:05.765 --> 00:06:07.900
Imagine if we do nothing about the climate!

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:12.939
Can you imagine the horror if the tap water
runs out in our houses or hospitals?

00:06:12.939 --> 00:06:18.277
Did we understand the beauty of life
and the fragility of life? 

00:06:18.277 --> 00:06:19.846
Isn’t it high time that we put finance
at the service of life?

00:06:20.747 --> 00:06:21.748
So how do we do it?

00:06:22.315 --> 00:06:25.151
How do we protect citizen's jobs and livelihoods 

00:06:25.151 --> 00:06:27.320
while addressing the biggest challenge

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:29.555
humanity has probably ever had to face? 

00:06:30.523 --> 00:06:33.292
For the Socialists and Democrats, the path is clear,

00:06:33.292 --> 00:06:35.361
it’s called the Green New Deal,

00:06:35.361 --> 00:06:37.764
with a just transition at its heart,

00:06:37.764 --> 00:06:39.899
to ensure that no one is left behind

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:44.137
when we re-build our economies and our societies
on a sustainable basis.

00:06:44.137 --> 00:06:45.605
As general rapporteur for the budget,

00:06:45.605 --> 00:06:47.774
I am fighting to have a very ambitious budget,

00:06:47.774 --> 00:06:52.245
a recovery and transformation budget
to be able to create 5 million jobs.

00:06:52.512 --> 00:06:54.080
If we want to win the climate battle,

00:06:54.213 --> 00:06:58.284
all houses, schools, retirement homes,
universities must be isolated.

00:06:58.284 --> 00:07:00.052
Public transport must be developed.

00:07:00.052 --> 00:07:01.387
We have to change our agricultural model.

00:07:01.387 --> 00:07:03.456
To do that, we need between €300 and
€500 billion each year.

00:07:04.357 --> 00:07:07.093
In the end, progressive ideas caught
the Commission’s ear

00:07:07.093 --> 00:07:10.663
and by the end of May it proposed
a comprehensive recovery plan:

00:07:10.663 --> 00:07:14.267
€750 billion to support the bloc's economies.

00:07:14.267 --> 00:07:16.335
These investments would come on top

00:07:16.335 --> 00:07:19.505
of an existing&nbsp;€1.1 trillion wide budgetary plan.

00:07:20.206 --> 00:07:22.909
The hardest hit sectors in the most affected regions

00:07:22.909 --> 00:07:24.744
must be provided with a life-line

00:07:24.744 --> 00:07:29.282
to ensure they don't have to fire workers
or close-down before recovery kicks in.

00:07:29.549 --> 00:07:31.951
The S&amp;D Group will continue putting pressure

00:07:31.951 --> 00:07:35.254
on EU leaders so that Europe can live up to its ambitions.

00:07:35.254 --> 00:07:36.389
Let's be honest about it,

00:07:36.389 --> 00:07:38.858
the problem is not to put more money on the table.

00:07:38.858 --> 00:07:40.960
The issue here is getting Europe to move forward.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:45.164
President Von Der Leyen,
as you can see, in this Parliament,

00:07:45.164 --> 00:07:49.836
you have an institution that is always ready to work
for the good of our citizens.

00:07:49.836 --> 00:07:53.105
Now, it's up to you to convince our friends in the EU Council.

00:07:53.105 --> 00:07:57.043
We owe it to future generations.  

00:07:58.744 --> 00:08:00.546
With the Coronavirus crisis,

00:08:00.546 --> 00:08:02.582
we've all been left in a state of shock.

00:08:03.015 --> 00:08:06.152
First and foremost was protecting people's health,

00:08:06.152 --> 00:08:08.888
supporting the most vulnerable in society, 

00:08:08.888 --> 00:08:13.626
and ensuring that workers will have
a job to return to after the end of the outbreak.

00:08:14.293 --> 00:08:17.697
But then, we also woke up to a new world of possibilities,

00:08:17.864 --> 00:08:21.100
working from home, experimenting new forms of solidarity.

00:08:21.801 --> 00:08:24.737
And when the clapping stops and when, bit-by-bit,

00:08:24.737 --> 00:08:28.674
that sense of normality comes back into our lives,
what will we do?

00:08:29.375 --> 00:08:33.412
Will we go back to our old ways,
or will we embrace real change?

00:08:34.080 --> 00:08:35.081
In this crisis, 

00:08:35.081 --> 00:08:38.284
the Progressives will have fought and pushed for a real change, 

00:08:38.284 --> 00:08:41.787
refusing to sit on the sidewalk of history while watching workers,

00:08:41.787 --> 00:08:44.690
businesses and our planet go bust.

00:08:44.690 --> 00:08:49.395
Now, it is time to be bold and innovative
and build a better future for all.

00:08:50.530 --> 00:08:53.165
Stay tuned for a next episode of "What's Left?"

00:08:53.266 --> 00:08:55.034
where we will explore in more depth

00:08:55.034 --> 00:08:58.104
how tomorrow's world could look like.

